Give GO_EXTLINK_ENABLED a third value, "darwin/amd64": external-link by default
when the target is darwin/amd64, and keep the automatic choice for every other
target. Go internal-links binaries without cgo, and the internal linker never
runs $CC -- so on OS X 10.9 a pure-Go program (or any CGO_ENABLED=0 build, or a
go test binary) gets no legacy-support shim, a 12.0 minimum-OS stamp, and dies
with "dyld: Symbol not found: _clock_gettime". GO_EXTLINK_ENABLED=1 fixes that
but also forces external linking for GOOS=linux and friends, which then fail
without a C toolchain for that OS. -linkmode and a link-time GO_EXTLINK_ENABLED
still override. Inert unless the toolchain was built with this value.

--- src/cmd/link/internal/ld/config.go.orig
+++ src/cmd/link/internal/ld/config.go
@@ -200,6 +200,18 @@
 		case "1":
 			ctxt.LinkMode = LinkExternal
 			via = "via GO_EXTLINK_ENABLED "
+		case "darwin/amd64":
+			// Mavericks: a darwin/amd64 binary -- pure Go included -- must go through $CC,
+			// whose wrapper links the 10.9 legacy-support shim (clock_gettime et al.) and
+			// stamps a 10.9 minimum OS; the internal linker can do neither. Other targets
+			// (GOOS=linux, ...) keep the automatic choice, so cross-OS builds still link
+			// without a C toolchain for that OS.
+			if buildcfg.GOOS == "darwin" && buildcfg.GOARCH == "amd64" {
+				ctxt.LinkMode = LinkExternal
+				via = "via GO_EXTLINK_ENABLED "
+				break
+			}
+			fallthrough
 		default:
 			preferExternal := len(preferlinkext) != 0
 			if preferExternal && ctxt.Debugvlog > 0 {
